Example:

```js
function x() {
  await Promise.resolve();
}

x();

```

Output: (which is not helpful)
```
error: Expected ";" but found "Promise"
  await Promise.resolve();
        ^
src/index.ts:2:9 23

error: Unexpected .
  await Promise.resolve();
               ^
src/index.ts:2:16 30
```

Trying to run 🐊[Putout](https://github.com/coderaiser/putout) code transformer with help of `Bun`, but see an error:

```sh
coderaiser@cloudcmd:~/putout/packages/putout$ bun bin/putout.mjs


error: Could not resolve: "fs/promises". Maybe you need to "bun install"?

} from 'fs/promises';
       ^
/Users/coderaiser/putout/packages/putout/bin/putout.mjs:6:8 66
```

Here is how `bin/putout.mjs` looks like:

```js
#!/usr/bin/env node

import {
    readFile,
    writeFile,
} from 'fs/promises';

import cli from '../lib/cli/index.js';

const {stdout} = process;
const write = stdout.write.bind(stdout);
const logError = console.error;

await cli({
    write,
    halt: process.exit,
    argv: process.argv.slice(2),
    log: console.log,
    logError,
    readFile,
    writeFile,
});
```

Also `bun bun bin/putout.mjs` shows:

```sh
error: Unexpected #!/usr/bin/env node
#!/usr/bin/env node
^
/Users/coderaiser/putout/packages/putout/bin/putout.mjs:1:1 0
```

For example,
```js
console.log(Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(globalThis, 'DOMException')); // => { writable: false, enumerable: true, configurable: false }
console.log(Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(globalThis, 'URL')); // => { writable: true, enumerable: true, configurable: false }
console.log(Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(globalThis, 'URLSearchParams')); // => { writable: false, enumerable: true, configurable: false }
```
\- they should be writable and configurable - and it breaks polyfills when they try to reinstall those properties with fixes.

### Version

0.1.3

### Platform

Microsoft Windows NT 10.0.22000.0 x64

### What steps will reproduce the bug?

runnable in node, bun, browser, etc
```ts
class ExtendedError extends Error {
  constructor() {
    super('Hello World!');
  }
}

function throwThing() {
  throw new ExtendedError();
}

throwThing();
```

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/24465214/178393748-6aab8cc5-c3b3-472b-91dc-33ab8b613bc2.png)


### Additional information

May be related to #185

1) Bun 0.1.6 prints no error message and successfully ends when an async function or IIFE throws an error that is not caught:

```js
import { promises as fs } from 'fs';
(async() => {
  console.log(await fs.readFile('./this_file_exists'));
  console.log(await fs.readFile('./this_file_does_not_exist'));
  console.log(await fs.readFile('./this_file_exists'));
})();
```

Testing:

```bash
$ touch this_file_exists
$ rm -f this_file_does_not_exist
$ bun error.mjs; echo $?
Uint8Array(0) [  ] ### <-- this is the first read result
0                  ### <-- process exited successfully
```

(to contrast, Node 18.7.0 exits with status code 1 and prints the error by default).

2) The `unhandledRejection` handler in `process` is not called:

```js
import process from 'process';
process.on('unhandledRejection', e => console.log(`!! ${e.message} !!`));

import { promises as fs } from 'fs';
(async() => {
  console.log(await fs.readFile('./this_file_exists'));
  console.log(await fs.readFile('./this_file_does_not_exist'));
  console.log(await fs.readFile('./this_file_exists'));
})();
```

(if the callback was called, the error message would be printed)

