### Additional information

The implementation works, the only thing that needs fixing is the type declaration.

It is declared in `globals.d.ts` (in bun-types) like so:
```ts
interface ArrayConstructor {
  fromAsync<T>(
    asyncItems: AsyncIterable<T> | Iterable<T> | ArrayLike<T>,
    mapfn?: (value: any, index: number) => any,
    thisArg?: any,
  ): Promise<T[]>;
}
```

I think it should be like that instead:
```ts
interface ArrayConstructor {
  fromAsync<T>(
    asyncItems: AsyncIterable<T> | Iterable<T> | ArrayLike<T>,
  ): Promise<Awaited<T>[]>;
  fromAsync<T, U>(
    asyncItems: AsyncIterable<T> | Iterable<T> | ArrayLike<T>,
    mapFn?: (value: T, index: number) => U,
    thisArg?: any,
  ): Promise<Awaited<U>[]>;
}
```

For more information about this method, read the mdn doc https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/fromAsync.

May I open a PR to fix the declaration and add tests?

### What is the problem this feature would solve?

Shell commands via bun.$ are always resolving so error handling needs to happen "manually" by looking at the errorCode in the response. The impact is that I cant use the expected way to deal with errors for async functions. 

It is not so much that error handling cant be done checking the value of errorCode, but it breaks with the philosophy of how things are done in JS.





### What is the feature you are proposing to solve the problem?

I propose changing the behaviour so results from bun.$ rejects when the value of errorCode is not-zero. This means that the below code would return "Command failed" as the binary echoXYZ could not be found. (This currently returns "Command ran OK" with Bun v 1.0.25)

```js
import { $ } from 'bun';

$`echoZYX Hello world`
	.then(o => console.log('Command ran OK: ', o))
	.catch(o => console.error('Command failed: ', o))
```


